            <ArticleTitle>Precision medicine path for prostate cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>786</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1158/2159-8290.CD-NB2015-089</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Almost 90% of patients with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer have a clinically actionable genetic alteration, according to a recently published study. Among these patients, researchers found that 65% had genetic changes that could be targeted by investigational or FDA-approved drugs currently used for other cancers, leading to new individualized treatment strategies.</AbstractText>
